      "number": 44,
      "title": "CIT-205 | CLASSROOM EXERCISE | GitHub Orientation Check-In",
      "body": "# CIT-205 Classroom Exercise — GitHub Orientation Check-In\n\n**Audience:** CIT-205 classroom participants  \n**Purpose:** Submission point for the GitHub Orientation and Documentation Discovery assignment  \n**Status:** Draft classroom exercise — use only when assigned by Professor Rinsem\n\nThis Issue is a classroom check-in. It is not an infrastructure mission, service ticket, or authorization to change SOTE systems.\n\n## Before posting\n\nComplete the student assignment linked from the classroom onboarding documentation. Explore the assigned repository pages and prepare your response before commenting.\n\n## Submit one comment\n\nCopy the template below into a new comment and replace each bracketed instruction with your own answer.\n\n```markdown\nCIT-205 GITHUB ORIENTATION CHECK-IN\n\n1. ACE current-state link:\n[Paste the link.]\n\n2. Status check:\n- One thing that currently exists or is confirmed:\n[Answer in one sentence.]\n- One thing currently in progress:\n[Answer in one sentence.]\n- One planned or target-state item:\n[Answer in one sentence.]\n\n3. Knowledge Article:\n- Article title and link:\n[Paste the title and link.]\n- What this article helps a technician do:\n[Answer in one or two sentences.]\n- One stop or escalation condition:\n[Answer in one sentence.]\n\n4. Documentation observation:\n- Page link:\n[Paste the link.]\n- What was clear, missing, outdated, or difficult to find:\n[Answer in one or two sentences.]\n- Suggested improvement:\n[Give one specific improvement.]\n\n5. GitHub navigation:\nOrganization → Repository → Folder → File\n[Explain in one sentence what each level means using the page you visited.]\n```\n\n## Submission rules\n\n- Post one comment from your own GitHub account.\n- Use complete, professional sentences.\n- Link the exact pages you used.\n- Base your status answers on repository evidence.\n- Do not post passwords, access tokens, private ticket information, grades, or other students' information.\n- Do not edit repository files, change labels, close this Issue, or comment on operational Mission Issues.\n- If the repository or comment box is unavailable, capture the screen and notify Professor Rinsem through the course channel.\n\n## Successful outcome\n\nA complete comment demonstrates that the student can:\n\n- enter the correct SOTE organization and repository;\n- navigate folders and Markdown files;\n- use exact links as evidence;\n- distinguish current, in-progress, and target-state information;\n- locate and interpret a Knowledge Article;\n- identify an escalation boundary;\n- provide useful documentation feedback.\n\nProfessor Rinsem will evaluate the assignment using the rubric in the student assignment document.",
      "updated_at": "2026-09-22T20:50:44Z",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/student-operated-technology-ecosystem/SOTE-framework/issues/44",
      "labels": []
