      "number": 13,
      "title": "OPS-004 | MISSION ISSUE | Mission Aggregation Forge | Workstation Aggregation",
      "body": "# OPS-004 MISSION ISSUE — Mission Aggregation Forge\n\n**Domain:** Infrastructure  \n**Execution Role:** SOTE Infrastructure Operator  \n**Mission Owner:** Professor Michael Rinsem  \n**Priority:** Follow-up Mission Control implementation work  \n**Objective:** Establish the workstation aggregation layer after the selected switch is validated and released from OPS-001.\n\n> **Live operational state:** GitHub fields are authoritative. **Assignees** identify the current Operator(s); the **status label** identifies current mission state; **comments** are the chronological work log. Do not duplicate live staffing or status in this mission charter.\n\n## Dependencies\n\nOPS-004 should not begin until OPS-001 has established and approved the aggregation-switch disposition.\n\n## What do I do first?\n\n1. Read this entire Mission Issue.\n2. Confirm the dependency above is satisfied.\n3. Complete the mission-specific pre-flight requirements in the runbook.\n4. Then open the OPS-004 Runbook: [Open OPS-004 RUNBOOK — Mission Aggregation Forge](https://github.com/student-operated-technology-ecosystem/SOTE-framework/blob/main/implementations/ccac/mission-control/operations/OPS-004_Mission-Aggregation-Forge.md)\n5. Post `MISSION STARTED` or `MISSION RESUMED — Current starting point` before field work begins.\n6. Use `CHECKPOINT` comments for meaningful progress and `BLOCKED` when work cannot safely or correctly continue.\n7. When execution is complete, request independent validation when another assigned Operator is available: `READY FOR VALIDATION — Work completed — Evidence/report location — Remaining concerns — @validator`.\n8. The validating Operator records `VALIDATION COMPLETE` or `VALIDATION FAILED` with findings.\n9. After successful validation, request Mission Owner review: `READY FOR REVIEW — Validation complete — Evidence/report location — Remaining concerns — @ProfRinsem`.\n\n## Operating Model\n\n**Mission Issue = stable mission charter and operational work queue.**  \n**Runbook = how to perform the technical work.**  \n**GitHub fields = live staffing and mission state.**  \n**Comments = chronological execution, validation, blocker, and handoff record.**\n\n## Planned Deliverables\n\n- Approved aggregation switch prepared and stable.\n- Workstation/patch-panel aggregation paths implemented from the approved port map.\n- VLAN/access/trunk baseline applied from approved design.\n- Connectivity and interface behavior validated.\n- Configuration and physical-path evidence preserved.\n- Follow-up work ticketed.\n- Independent validation recorded when another qualified Operator is available.\n\n## Safety / Stop-Work\n\nDo not execute while device disposition, destructive-action authorization, port/VLAN/uplink design, or operational impact is uncertain. Stop and document any condition that makes the next action unsafe, destructive, or insufficiently understood.\n\n## Completion\n\nAfter execution and validation are complete, post:\n\n`READY FOR REVIEW — Validation complete — Evidence/report location — Remaining concerns — @ProfRinsem`\n\nThe Mission Owner verifies the work log, evidence, validation, and follow-up Issues, then either requests additional work or closes the Mission Issue.\n\n## Change Control\n\nIf field experience shows that the runbook or other controlled SOTE documentation is incorrect or incomplete, do not silently rewrite the official document. Record the finding in this Mission Issue and use the established branch, pull request, review, and merge workflow.\n\n## Renumbering Record\n\nThis mission was renumbered from OPS-003 to OPS-004 when Mission Server Intake and Validation became the official semester OPS-003.",
